{"name":"Snow Flurry","hex":"#eaf7c9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"snow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The partly frozen, crystalline state of water that falls from the atmosphere as precipitation in flakes; also, the falling of such flakes; and the accumulation of them on the ground or on objects as a white layer.\n\n An instance of the falling of snow (etymology 1, noun sense 1); a snowfall; also, a snowstorm.","An instance of the falling of snow (etymology 1, noun sense 1); a snowfall; also, a snowstorm."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Preceded by the dummy subject it: to have snow (noun etymology 1, noun sense 1) fall from the atmosphere.","Of a thing: to fall like snow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/snow"},{"term":"flurry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light, brief snowfall; a shower of snow.","A sudden and brief blast or gust; a light, temporary breeze."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To agitate, bewilder, fluster.","To move or fall in a flurry."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flurry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Snow flurry","blocks":[{"text":"A snow flurry is a light snowfall that results in little or no snow accumulation. The US National Weather Service defines snow flurries as intermittent light snow that produces no measurable precipitation. In contrast, bursts of snowfall that do result in measurable snow accumulation are called snow showers. Environment Canada uses a different definition for flurries, approximately equivalent to 'snow shower'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/20080307_Niagara_Square_to_Lafayette_Square.JPG/330px-20080307_Niagara_Square_to_Lafayette_Square.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snow_flurry","wikidata":"Q2247306"},{"title":"Snow Flurry (design)","blocks":[{"text":"The Snow Flurry design was used by American artist Alexander Calder for at least seven mobiles between 1948 and 1959. A monumental design composed of white disks of varying sizes are connected on different branches and levels to reflect a snow flurry in Calder's distinct Modernist style."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Rafaga_de_nieve_%282037762977%29.jpg/330px-Rafaga_de_nieve_%282037762977%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snow_Flurry_(design)","wikidata":"Q93632932"},{"title":"Snow","blocks":[{"text":"Snow is a form of solid atmospheric precipitation consisting of individual ice crystals that grow while suspended in the air—usually within clouds—and then fall, accumulating on the ground, where they undergo further changes. It consists of frozen crystalline water throughout its life cycle, starting when, under suitable conditions, the ice crystals form in the atmosphere, increase to millimeter size, precipitate and accumulate on surfaces, then metamorphose in place, and ultimately melt, slide, or sublimate away."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg/330px-CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snow","wikidata":"Q7561"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white","wikidata":"Q7460350"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Two-oh-six_three-sixty-five_%285391425836%29.jpg/500px-Two-oh-six_three-sixty-five_%285391425836%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Two-oh-six_three-sixty-five_%285391425836%29.jpg/1280px-Two-oh-six_three-sixty-five_%285391425836%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Two-oh-six_three-sixty-five_%285391425836%29.jpg/500px-Two-oh-six_three-sixty-five_%285391425836%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A snow flurry","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATwo-oh-six_three-sixty-five_(5391425836).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/FAU%2C_Ciudad_Universitaria.jpg/500px-FAU%2C_Ciudad_Universitaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/FAU%2C_Ciudad_Universitaria.jpg/1280px-FAU%2C_Ciudad_Universitaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/FAU%2C_Ciudad_Universitaria.jpg/500px-FAU%2C_Ciudad_Universitaria.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Ráfaga de nieve overhangs a balcony in the Central University of Venezuela's Faculty of Architecture and Urbanism. Two other Calder sculptures are on the balcony.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFAU%2C_Ciudad_Universitaria.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Feb242007_blizzard.gif/500px-Feb242007_blizzard.gif?